  "query_preview": "..\nRest of Today through Saturday...\n\nDisorganized upper trof will be over the area through tomorrow\nkeeping cool and unsettled conditions over the area. Hopes that \nthis system would organize a bit better and bring more widespread \nprecipitation to the region have faded however. The system\ncontinues splitting in two and the more impactful portion is now \nexpected to stay far enough south of the area to limit \nprecipitation potential to a tenth of an inch or less for most of \nthe area, outside of favored upslope northerly slopes and areas \nalong the southeast MT/WY border. \n\nEven upslope precipitation potential and amounts have trended\ndownward over the past 24 hours as the upper dynamics piece has\nweakened with the core of the system staying further south. That\nsaid there should still be enough snow in the Beartooth and Red\nLodge foothills above 5000 feet to support the going Winter\nWeather Advisory there, but there is going to be a sharp\naccumulating snow line not too far north of Nye and Red Lodge \nbased on the latest model trends. \n\nWinds will remain breezy to locally windy at times through Monday\nwith the storm system such as it is situated over the region. With\nthe reinforcing cold front this afternoon and evening, gusts in\nthe 25 to 30 mph range will be widespread, with gusts up to 40 mph\nin the western and southern foothills. \n\nStorm system exits to the east on Tuesday leaving modest\nnorthwesterly flow over the area for the rest of the work week. A\ndisturbance on Wednesday is looking a bit more organized in some\nof the models and may bring a more organized period of showers to\nthe region Wednesday afternoon into Thursday. Current\nprobabilities for a quarter inch or more with this system are over\n40% for area mountains and foothills, and over 25% for lower\nelevations west of Rosebud county. It will be cold enough for high\nelevation snowfall with this system as probabilities for 6+ inches\nof snow accumulation are around 20-25% for the Beartooth/Absaroka\nand Bighorn mountains. \n\nOtherwise, precipitation chances remain low through the week \nwith temperatures trending near to slightly above normal in the \nmid 60s to lower 70s. Upper flow backs to the west on Saturday \nwhich will help boost forecast highs into the 80s with plenty of \nsun. Chambers",
